#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166536 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166912 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5802583-df2c-5f08-bf9f-8b36247e34d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/id #uuid "2e24d040-266c-5ba5-bb2b-9918c22eb48e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21596" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?\n\n![](images/04/040ac76a28687db1c99d3379242b726a85aa4baf269a7c6d35d9662af1d3bd78.png)" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/0c/0c10626785034f566b65778fad63032e3a9ad8cc2c884bc3da3e88fdff3db50b.svg)\nwhere ![](images/18/18d25c282ee921144387d9c30e19a461746271a44cab929ab8075190b0f5c16e.svg)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/e9/e99452a0ae70ad466de402da561590e4fc3b0de089fa0f74818a1986cadfbd42.svg)\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-6$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/50/50b227b577144e091543a383e38d0d74a02193b8372fec1eddcfa75d06d3878b.svg)\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](images/0e/0ee79c93c2e488563f7515fb0ec6b30d5cb2b41795d23f0b9d4699338edcc92c.svg)\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/f1/f10a5247f1d1b5df56adab19d1dd64e9edbc04fb5615db365f62b1eebda40dc7.svg)\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{4t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/51/517dcfcf2467960dd5d35d9676c86c4bf0f66e0c4f13ccd99e862816efed675e.svg)\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since $e^{-6t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/91/91c074766b92790a0e78b768b0290d7723617d6ed30e51e842501cad63e58a44.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166537 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d81d981f-ab55-5637-bff5-ae47633b0b79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the System Given a Phase Portrait"}] :question/id #uuid "d4296f42-4f86-5ef8-813e-261afa85b829" :question/math-academy-id "e-21597" :question/problem "![](images/25/25d4f77a3f596202229581e06d88a9e3bdd84dc6b8b7e14b6f46b3b3d2f125d2.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?\n\nI. ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6y(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6y(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\nIV. ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/50/50b547a3223fe85dde50b06d78ee35bfff49d95c43a4ebadcc2d1007ac041eb1.svg)\nwhere ![](images/ee/ee1bb1b6a9869493ae3bcfaa42d63d07b6054aaa19f25c9fa8a2665e9642e0be.svg)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/85/853be349a48d204b073b1ad3aaa709df082bd7fa5163aec09d5364836918a9e6.svg)\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](images/59/5903874b33d77f242d725ad16fffef59ee24e1bde4210128ed590031ddc43e99.svg)\nmust approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_1 < 0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](images/85/856d5a86b76c30e0601803216465f29a1e3bd7fb993a58ee6ba85ba7c0627fd9.svg)\nmust also approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_2 < 0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies $\\lambda_1 < 0$ and $\\lambda_2 < 0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166540 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166915 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24bd917d-c4b3-5bf5-9426-a87a579b03c1" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Decoupled Systems of Linear Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "4926ad20-fc6b-5a7d-80ee-aa39421c174d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21607" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nInterpret the stability of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the equilibrium point (the origin) for the given decoupled system of linear differential equations." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/73/73afc7cad8d4018e66ce31d26275c6d3238e0b50bfc85c65c84abb3d1d9c3c3c.svg)\nwhere ![](images/37/375392fe3e7223efb30963c4f51b76a08f868bdde210574bb8fb0f2e53f925be.svg)\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=2$ with the eigenvector ![](images/b8/b8dce83f78184c194c43b2cf2b4148549a4ff0504a0f663f6a338a8cca6dec2f.svg) The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/79/796f9e0b1c5698e87439547b5c84e00d492a6c7893cfd6adb793bd0639147d25.svg)\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{unstable}}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=4$ with the eigenvector ![](images/f9/f9da7bff2e0d90f112ce26291e3c1074e766e141bdae4198870d49136ed6706a.svg) The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/9d/9d8a16728992f0ce055be81c84ae0e905417e2ed74c4e659b277ab7f4226e90a.svg)\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{unstable}}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{an unstable node (source)}}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/2b/2b79201d15fe5d2a9896cdee09c0474b97bb933d75fcf478c6fdb37cb4d16742.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166542 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ee85e-c6ce-5c8e-bb14-d0a32d6810e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait Given a System: Advanced Cases"}] :question/id #uuid "74560a13-4a23-553d-8c8d-6c040f03a957" :question/math-academy-id "e-21633" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?\n\n![](images/47/470210fb9509e87c3e51a990bac7483e5862d2f37afdce9823279838e8391592.png)" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/cc/cc4019b4978cb96b931632ee2b6bf318b5eda46f4d9a73d252e76c7572285517.svg)\nwhere ![](images/45/458d9c56e8c1857546e7e112a9ad084fe7832781d448340ccfc141438a6eee78.svg)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-1$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/d5/d504ae47c4cfcd428a362d590d23713844d9ead5df792086a3bab07d41573bd8.svg)\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/92/9210f84189ff4a94c1a1dded19ff6e409c75be9d4e27f17d4d1a4d9ff44fcc0b.svg)\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](images/38/38e227b8bac9c4cb2ae93e2b29e1ca70576383a176dcf193350a9b464a4dd88f.svg)\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/7e/7ef739bcc9a338d9535ab130a3bbe1cd46204bf7d8dbd55a49244abe9764a04a.svg)\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{-t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/92/924dcd7002fa679bf3a0021099928c45fba82a5bf54ba157a21d846eb1587640.svg)\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since $e^{-4t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/c5/c5b1aa6ccde39c56b503e2d59a56e2ac8058b08495567f4e2cb40f6a056faedd.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t) = c_1e^{-t}$ and $y(t)=c_2e^{-4t}$ provided that $c_1,c_2 \\neq 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-t})^{′}}=\\frac{-4{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}}{-{c}_{1}{e}^{-t}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}}{{c}_{1}}{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/6c/6c3af2fe68c4bcddbbfd9f13eb84e73014511374c0adc570084f9c92773d31e0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166915 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24bd917d-c4b3-5bf5-9426-a87a579b03c1" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Decoupled Systems of Linear Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25c968e3-c729-5639-a3ca-cee68536dead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302699} {:answer/id #uuid "7394b45d-420f-52bf-bbc5-96da7f80405a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303156} {:answer/id #uuid "b9434a51-00db-5650-a51f-ba47f7ee2919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7394b45d-420f-52bf-bbc5-96da7f80405a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303156} :answer-field/id #uuid "18d2b03a-cb1a-519b-9c31-766de086e40a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304136} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8ffdda8-340a-5a7f-8516-875ce2fdcb92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303498} {:answer/id #uuid "f45f4210-507a-5d8f-9dae-805885e0b36f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8ffdda8-340a-5a7f-8516-875ce2fdcb92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303498} :answer-field/id #uuid "a18eae7b-a5bc-50c8-a851-a35904c65972" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304513} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f4041ca-7f05-5692-9e7a-a49f5d0072f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302556} {:answer/id #uuid "e58ab17d-33c7-558f-9231-284eeefb8c26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f4041ca-7f05-5692-9e7a-a49f5d0072f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302556} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcbdc676-89c3-590c-9ac8-13ae5deba4e5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6644c70-dc61-58f2-81c3-a23eae94c4cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-331517" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/09/0948dac4f8a192018baee1339d7334d573592d7b7bf4383c1297cc2431e51201.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ee85e-c6ce-5c8e-bb14-d0a32d6810e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait Given a System: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04a2b290-8039-5ca4-94f1-b39d0c36e25a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/eb/eba955015a4b919c65dc24facb0baa6e745df134feac32feeae07c99d40ff4ec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302486} {:answer/id #uuid "3c581c58-1d13-52d9-9e42-5b001ed96302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/09/0948dac4f8a192018baee1339d7334d573592d7b7bf4383c1297cc2431e51201.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302831} {:answer/id #uuid "57cd8149-e3f4-5a37-93f4-a3a274e3a24a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/87/8715fffa8fec9468edef2194b75842cb79de3bc9a7db6c0d8d23f44c6eb5368b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302991} {:answer/id #uuid "bab62c71-181b-5361-b434-80130ab31878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d7/d70a32516a4c051a8f4dc7460a313afe817fde14896cb108d5978858b58864ff.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303609} {:answer/id #uuid "cc0ba666-971f-56d1-93d5-ff65be164c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7e/7e48266d30ae7c204b25e6eddca968d1b4ce9cd8e1c7b19cd20247fa66070f0b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04a2b290-8039-5ca4-94f1-b39d0c36e25a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/eb/eba955015a4b919c65dc24facb0baa6e745df134feac32feeae07c99d40ff4ec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302486} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ccddb7d-5291-5236-a231-6e5f34e99835" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f707130-0164-506c-911a-bb075d503cd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-332271" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/f5/f54544342b11a60ec784160f582b5bdd03a28abee01767a732bef6ac03d9ec62.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{3t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{3t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{2t})^{′}}=\\frac{3{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}}{2{c}_{1}{e}^{2t}}=\\frac{3{c}_{2}}{2{c}_{1}}{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to \\pm \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/eb/eba955015a4b919c65dc24facb0baa6e745df134feac32feeae07c99d40ff4ec.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167215 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166912 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5802583-df2c-5f08-bf9f-8b36247e34d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "101fadb7-e4a4-5775-a692-421ec4666605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9e/9eb64f436febd0b3cbc108920317ca8eed58139f453347033f1bdedd74f08f42.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302562} {:answer/id #uuid "18572fb1-6da2-5ca5-a7d8-8e72f0a57a95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/37/374e12736ee4cf016f75711f67b7d6d1696f48cc6288f13df236812998c4202c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302618} {:answer/id #uuid "23281918-757d-59a9-936e-99e2d29adc32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a5/a52abbca3f2186e1a5d6ca6c33646718484312e3aa1cc066e3c0f8a1b5f1152a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302679} {:answer/id #uuid "76e633ec-8b8c-54e1-a839-d9e107941080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bd/bd15cec76717d417e1ee906c10abaddc77c7095d4e20c95a90afed21a1592c01.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303189} {:answer/id #uuid "86f7dd7e-15f1-5819-8a6c-7d22f4e5e029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/37/3784f65f97466e17d857a66ca17e69173de72e9608111c839e989cfff3fe10e0.png" 