estion/id #uuid "14b93aa5-65d3-5ddc-9724-ab937ea4f1a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-332678" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-5t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n\\\n\n![](images/3f/3facfdf59a92a9202d834cd77d14e009c4ec69c5f1b7bd5f12bd17ea1bbc338b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166912 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5802583-df2c-5f08-bf9f-8b36247e34d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50c7bc7d-0232-5fe1-a6d4-6f9a22440550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7c/7c483bbf926df156b7f85fbedcbb6ab18571ff4f544416cd8ea554b70b59a589.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391045} {:answer/id #uuid "9c4028ce-9f71-5bd4-ad6b-47272c4ba666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/27/27c6bf8d11d3de50beac351f88104df58cac31053eb6ecfc3a35ce77ccd8600b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391046} {:answer/id #uuid "f48f6682-16a0-5a4e-b948-d2adbb5ee402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/01/01d93cb2e68123b061b057ce30b94bcce44401b2d0fc0c566b3c33478c07fb92.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391047} {:answer/id #uuid "4d70c978-6c43-538a-a72f-3e3d73db9a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d604781deed3206caf0a3bf6afa857c00beca9d532a26dedef5c717b4f684225.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391048} {:answer/id #uuid "e571bbe0-b813-51a0-a84b-7bcbc9e1fb37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/38/389d3cfc92ece3a63300767f92c34c11dccec981ac43f3f66a2ced02906f8a73.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d70c978-6c43-538a-a72f-3e3d73db9a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d604781deed3206caf0a3bf6afa857c00beca9d532a26dedef5c717b4f684225.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391048} :answer-field/id #uuid "23f7e5e5-ba46-5c75-bde7-3c2b00f02f16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f7b6c7b-9bbd-58e1-9f4f-ab6d8faeb7f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-332683" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-7y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-7t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/d6/d604781deed3206caf0a3bf6afa857c00beca9d532a26dedef5c717b4f684225.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d81d981f-ab55-5637-bff5-ae47633b0b79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the System Given a Phase Portrait"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4637eefd-6ffa-5302-a1bf-464d7d3655e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302900} {:answer/id #uuid "bd36e0e8-924e-5434-885b-28aa9e03a597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303617} {:answer/id #uuid "c2e1bd02-e825-5c61-8342-967c4baa3a77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303650} {:answer/id #uuid "c42e116e-7a9f-57e0-871d-2274f8465da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303662} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d93aa3-5f2a-5d35-85b1-a93ba6faafae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0d93aa3-5f2a-5d35-85b1-a93ba6faafae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303743} :answer-field/id #uuid "39400a04-b082-53e0-a216-5351b940bb0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f20c0d44-466d-592f-adfd-5535d1164b77" :question/math-academy-id "q-332705" :question/problem "![](images/98/98ffa8fec669ce09d22b5b904b710632314f7b22e0eb5098719c41147c186975.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}>0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305100 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d81d981f-ab55-5637-bff5-ae47633b0b79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the System Given a Phase Portrait"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11c5b7a3-26ff-50f5-94e9-6d8844fb678c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302575} {:answer/id #uuid "2f3d43fe-787c-5177-bbac-12bebe043929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302751} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf0d4ee-e24f-5d9c-b2b7-0d001e462222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302835} {:answer/id #uuid "92871808-ead1-5f60-af6a-dad167814456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303367} {:answer/id #uuid "f06a5725-94e7-58bc-b5b3-9291ccbbab16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92871808-ead1-5f60-af6a-dad167814456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303367} :answer-field/id #uuid "11c07c39-3607-539e-8608-e01288afb165" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e70744b-cd4d-5289-8a1e-db4fcf31bc45" :question/math-academy-id "q-332707" :question/problem "![](images/52/5243b22ebef45709f214ac2422cfb72528f24fd5ef51bd38aa3fc0ff40f1888d.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}>0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305102 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d81d981f-ab55-5637-bff5-ae47633b0b79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the System Given a Phase Portrait"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0857aa6f-f632-50ec-bdeb-a7c114223d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302512} {:answer/id #uuid "542070f4-ea1c-5b48-ae85-80ed1dddb750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302980} {:answer/id #uuid "74b5102f-6daf-5f4f-8ac1-e4938678a70d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-7x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303167} {:answer/id #