\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}<0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420024 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d81d981f-ab55-5637-bff5-ae47633b0b79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the System Given a Phase Portrait"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ff71483-521d-5e25-81d5-dd0d7513eda3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419966} {:answer/id #uuid "300406ec-c504-5656-92c4-2b5564e19ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419967} {:answer/id #uuid "46bd9ac2-d6f5-56fc-b2fb-750eb4c39fd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-7y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419969} {:answer/id #uuid "d71c1d37-aaf1-51f8-838a-ad2cd869f1c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-7y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419993} {:answer/id #uuid "d96d1f17-303b-53d2-98c9-2a8785c1198c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=7x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186