o, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}<0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-7y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308404 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ee85e-c6ce-5c8e-bb14-d0a32d6810e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait Given a System: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "085486de-a0c1-555a-9181-b4b425a4fe03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ef/ef7ed85b18002aeec299826750ecc6306c90db8bf2c9bbd9bd8173af0ee9ea41.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305229} {:answer/id #uuid "6560fb87-3260-5d7b-9f4a-a880c5cbdb8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b9/b930f98928e3c4af06434c720d953ad0fcee17500113ed9a72492eda4219a7fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306127} {:answer/id #uuid "a66b10fb-fc03-5ace-b7f9-3b049191a810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0b/0b2cfbe650613293fe807be4ff7e38fa6f10b2e792dae8f7f4b78f27560f59d7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306718} {:answer/id #uuid "bc1a1504-015b-5c38-b69c-6616892e04ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e8/e8110ea6d6a113e72e35502030afb495a23bc81106d64874582d03aae81b7c73.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306904} {:answer/id #uuid "c3faec70-b3c6-579b-bf25-6087a8f1f205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ff/ff9ffa100793101811d8aa6e0e95587ec912ef1e3f64d59fbac617a521479a5d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc1a1504-015b-5c38-b69c-6616892e04ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e8/e8110ea6d6a113e72e35502030afb495a23bc81106d64874582d03aae81b7c73.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306904} :answer-field/id #uuid "8230b11d-8bb8-5c4c-873b-8b24da7d6f03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf46b344-09be-5328-8704-8fe72b540c14" :question/math-academy-id "q-333184" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{6t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/24/2473117cdca4318187b94374820c0a12576e998e7f631a6e7bf51e56c985be43.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{6t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{6t})^{′}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}}{6{c}_{1}{e}^{6t}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/e8/e8110ea6d6a113e72e35502030afb495a23bc81106d64874582d03aae81b7c73.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ee85e-c6ce-5c8e-bb14-d0a32d6810e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait Given a System: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "536c8ba2-9a76-58ee-a671-8d410c7b610d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bf/bff6e7e5f4772441cdc81c809eadc377d3c2c43bd653d0c5c4f7f306adf79a5e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419971} {:answer/id #uuid "7adc6eff-a523-5b7e-8d97-de46f768fe8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/30/30d392d02e60c7bb8d76f7d4773a1d016d7cfa6343c893fb644428fde58571bd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419975} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc531d0-6c2d-5648-b378-65082454a6cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/07/0781cf2ca92efb10c050102b8dd034e004bb8da29b743fb154e2cc6aaf374c28.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419990} {:answer/id #uuid "d44e5877-6f35-50c1-a9af-6f5adefc791b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/90/90addbe477ec66925586a5f8da3921dcaa6e22ed9ea4c1fb65f8b91095ad4850.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419992} {:answer/id #uuid "e83d540c-00e0-5931-b515-134723ba6e69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/13/139efbf21eb0cf93de2b041a63da84ac802898a810031c89471c620f0cb98ed6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "536c8ba2-9a76-58ee-a671-8d410c7b610d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bf/bff6e7e5f4772441cdc81c809eadc377d3c2c43bd653d0c5c4f7f306adf79a5e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419971} :answer-field/id #uuid "0521532e-d96e-5d0f-ae24-3ae545170424" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "92828ad5-f63d-58d9-843f-369518e02ad5" :question/math-academy-id "q-333188" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/36/362ee182f39f9d5cc1e7597f7e836a6ce802b4762a03c347c5701c0830f3d523.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-3t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-5t})^{′}}=\\frac{-3{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}}{-5{c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}}=\\frac{3{c}_{2}}{5{c}_{1}}{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to \\pm \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/bf/bff6e7e5f4772441cdc81c809eadc377d3c2c43bd653d0c5c4f7f306adf79a5e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420026 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ee85e-c6ce-5c8e-bb14-d0a32d6810e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait Given a System: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8691b44a-b7c9-5764-a3ef-11415f5cb459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ba/badef95926770dce7b4b7bb7dd239d1d730acf1839b4af470a97365343bece58.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419976} {:answer/id #uuid "ac18ff8b-9d13-557c-8b66-d6ab72ff43d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/11/11484704b873e4f4cb449e8eb452e0e4e2ceb71d9f2918b052d5f18cbdd7ca7b.p