\\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/29/29fba4c8d3fa024689c550da1bf54a50a81d73114ea1b2fa3a566966e9cb0692.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166915 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24bd917d-c4b3-5bf5-9426-a87a579b03c1" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Decoupled Systems of Linear Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d7c7175-1a31-52fd-87d7-275a7f4142ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419965} {:answer/id #uuid "e714798a-3dc6-56b8-9999-84e5040e8804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419998} {:answer/id #uuid "ee0863b6-4436-5460-b07a-e30f587eb499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e714798a-3dc6-56b8-9999-84e5040e8804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419998} :answer-field/id #uuid "1af6e17b-3145-51a4-aeaa-9807cc90bb62" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420008} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "241c7c5a-1a6e-5bba-bd0d-81a296eeafbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419963} {:answer/id #uuid "dd242aa8-4bfe-5d24-b506-eff53329bbb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "241c7c5a-1a6e-5bba-bd0d-81a296eeafbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419963} :answer-field/id #uuid "e14fbafa-f0bc-5fe2-bb94-563a8f48a64a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420016} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e34e418-2968-5992-a5b6-638f9e13fb76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419970} {:answer/id #uuid "a5882e06-57dc-58e3-8a32-7bc5eed48bf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5882e06-57dc-58e3-8a32-7bc5eed48bf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419981} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6148daf-4057-5d9a-8a64-641f2d763dc1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b822a97-2fd4-5c06-914f-271b7726501f" :question/math-academy-id "q-333226" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1c/1c4133695eeb868f563f80021755fea75bef622e1ad46cbbf6daff1c953cb075.png)"}]}
